"""
Candidate benchmarks NOT YET plotted in aggregated.json -- a decision list,
not a data pull. Consolidated and deduplicated from three independent
research passes (missing-benchmarks-audit-{claude,codex,copilot}.md), each
of which did real web research (official repos, papers, leaderboards) to
find gaps in the 111-benchmark catalog.

This file does not fetch or invent any (date, model, score) data. Its job
is to let the user browse candidates and pick which ones are worth a real
follow-up research-and-plot pass, informed by an honest feasibility read
per candidate:

  "high"   - confirmed active/maintained public leaderboard or bulk dataset
             with multiple models and dates -- a real data pull is very
             likely to succeed the same way the existing 111 did.
  "medium" - a real paper/repo with a comparative results table across
             several models, but no continuously maintained leaderboard --
             a data pull would likely yield a handful of snapshot points,
             not a rich time series.
  "low"    - foundational/historical dataset, largely superseded or no
             longer reported in current frontier-model releases -- data
             may exist but is stale (pre-2023) and of limited value for
             the plateau/saturation question this project is built around.
  "not_a_benchmark" - a framework, dataset without comparative evaluation,
             or an environment that other benchmarks are built on top of
             (e.g. BrowserGym, Inspect) -- flagged so it isn't mistaken for
             a candidate with its own plottable score.

Each entry's `flagged_by` records which audit(s) independently surfaced it
(agreement across audits is itself a confidence signal).
"""

